#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Proof that todo.py still agrees with the board.

The `repeat:` grammar now exists three times: in JavaScript in
kanban/index.html, in Python here in kanban/todo.py, and a third time in
pa-checkin/scripts/check_todo.py, which is zipped into a skill and so cannot
import either of the others. Three copies of one set of rules is a standing
invitation to drift, and drift here is quiet — a meeting rolls to the wrong
Thursday and nothing complains.

The table below was generated by running the board's own occurrenceFrom and
occurrenceAfter, so it is the board's answers rather than a second opinion.
Thirty tag forms, each asked for its next occurrence from seven days in each of
fourteen months, and then walked twelve occurrences from a fixed anchor. Between
them that covers month-end clamping, February, the nth working day landing later
in a short month, the December rollover, and the `/n` intervals — which are
invisible to occurrenceFrom and only show up in the walk.

It also pins the working calendar. Those dates are worked out from the rules
rather than typed in, which is what stops the list expiring — and also what makes
a quiet mistake possible, since nothing complains if a rule is subtly wrong. The
two tables below are the published holidays for 2026 and 2027.

They were checked against the sources on 4 Sep 2026, and `--online` runs that
same check again: gov.uk's own feed for England and Wales, and Nager.Date for
Portugal, which has no official feed. Every England-and-Wales date from 2019 to
2028 agreed, apart from the five one-off events no rule can produce — the VE Day
move, the Jubilee, the State Funeral and the Coronation — which are written into
todo.UK_EXTRA and checked here too. Both Portuguese years agreed exactly.

The offline tables are what run by default. A test that needs the network is a
test that fails on a train.

    python3 kanban/test_todo.py             the pinned tables
    python3 kanban/test_todo.py --online    and again against the sources
"""

